NNAAMMEE
       groupadd - create a new group

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ggrroouuppaadddd [_O_P_T_I_O_N_S] _N_E_W_G_R_O_U_P

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The ggrroouuppaadddd command creates a new group account using the values
       specified on the command line plus the default values from the system.
       The new group will be entered into the system files as needed.

       Groupnames may contain only lower and upper case letters, digits,
       underscores, or dashes. They can end with a dollar sign. Dashes are not
       allowed at the beginning of the groupname. Fully numeric groupnames and
       groupnames . or .. are also disallowed.

       On Debian, the only constraints are that groupnames must neither start
       with a dash ('-') nor plus ('+') nor tilde ('~') nor contain a colon
       (':'), a comma (','), or a whitespace (space:' ', end of line: '\n',
       tabulation: '\t', etc.).

       Groupnames may only be up to 32 characters long.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       The options which apply to the ggrroouuppaadddd command are:

       --ff, ----ffoorrccee
           This option causes the command to simply exit with success status
           if the specified group already exists. When used with --gg, and the
           specified GID already exists, another (unique) GID is chosen (i.e.
           --gg is turned off).

       --gg, ----ggiidd _G_I_D
           The numerical value of the group's ID.  _G_I_D must be unique, unless
           the --oo option is used. The value must be non-negative. The default
           is to use the smallest ID value greater than or equal to GGIIDD__MMIINN
           and greater than every other group.

           See also the --rr option and the GGIIDD__MMAAXX description.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help message and exit.

       --KK, ----kkeeyy _K_E_Y=_V_A_L_U_E
           Overrides /etc/login.defs defaults (GID_MIN, GID_MAX and others).
           Multiple --KK options can be specified.

           Example: --KK _G_I_D___M_I_N=_1_0_0  --KK _G_I_D___M_A_X=_4_9_9

           Note: --KK _G_I_D___M_I_N=_1_0,_G_I_D___M_A_X=_4_9_9 doesn't work yet.

       --oo, ----nnoonn--uunniiqquuee
           permits the creation of a group with an already used numerical ID.
           As a result, for this _G_I_D, the mapping towards group _N_E_W_G_R_O_U_P may
           not be unique.

       --pp, ----ppaasssswwoorrdd _P_A_S_S_W_O_R_D
           defines an initial password for the group account. PASSWORD is
           expected to be encrypted, as returned by ccrryypptt (3).

           Without this option, the group account will be locked and with no
           password defined, i.e. a single exclamation mark in the respective
           field of ths system account file /etc/group or /etc/gshadow.

           NNoottee:: This option is not recommended because the password (or
           encrypted password) will be visible by users listing the processes.

           You should make sure the password respects the system's password
           policy.

       --rr, ----ssyysstteemm
           Create a system group.

           The numeric identifiers of new system groups are chosen in the
           SSYYSS__GGIIDD__MMIINN-SSYYSS__GGIIDD__MMAAXX range, defined in login.defs, instead of
           GGIIDD__MMIINN-GGIIDD__MMAAXX.

       --RR, ----rroooott _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R
           Apply changes in the _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R directory and use the configuration
           files from the _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R directory. Only absolute paths are
           supported.

       --PP, ----pprreeffiixx _P_R_E_F_I_X___D_I_R
           Apply changes to configuration files under the root filesystem
           found under the directory _P_R_E_F_I_X___D_I_R. This option does not chroot
           and is intended for preparing a cross-compilation target. Some
           limitations: NIS and LDAP users/groups are not verified. PAM
           authentication is using the host files. No SELINUX support.

       --UU, ----uusseerrss
           A list of usernames to add as members of the group.

           The default behavior (if the --gg, --NN, and --UU options are not
           specified) is defined by the UUSSEERRGGRROOUUPPSS__EENNAABB variable in
           /etc/login.defs.

CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN
       The following configuration variables in /etc/login.defs change the
       behavior of this tool:

       GGIIDD__MMAAXX (number), GGIIDD__MMIINN (number)
           Range of group IDs used for the creation of regular groups by
           uusseerraadddd, ggrroouuppaadddd, or nneewwuusseerrss.

           The default value for GGIIDD__MMIINN (resp.  GGIIDD__MMAAXX) is 1000 (resp.
           60000).

       MMAAXX__MMEEMMBBEERRSS__PPEERR__GGRROOUUPP (number)
           Maximum members per group entry. When the maximum is reached, a new
           group entry (line) is started in /etc/group (with the same name,
           same password, and same GID).

           The default value is 0, meaning that there are no limits in the
           number of members in a group.

           This feature (split group) permits to limit the length of lines in
           the group file. This is useful to make sure that lines for NIS
           groups are not larger than 1024 characters.

           If you need to enforce such limit, you can use 25.

           Note: split groups may not be supported by all tools (even in the
           Shadow toolsuite). You should not use this variable unless you
           really need it.

       SSYYSS__GGIIDD__MMAAXX (number), SSYYSS__GGIIDD__MMIINN (number)
           Range of group IDs used for the creation of system groups by
           uusseerraadddd, ggrroouuppaadddd, or nneewwuusseerrss.

           The default value for SSYYSS__GGIIDD__MMIINN (resp.  SSYYSS__GGIIDD__MMAAXX) is 101
           (resp.  GGIIDD__MMIINN-1).

FFIILLEESS
       /etc/group
           Group account information.

       /etc/gshadow
           Secure group account information.

       /etc/login.defs
           Shadow password suite configuration.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
       You may not add a NIS or LDAP group. This must be performed on the
       corresponding server.

       If the groupname already exists in an external group database such as
       NIS or LDAP, ggrroouuppaadddd will deny the group creation request.

EEXXIITT VVAALLUUEESS
       The ggrroouuppaadddd command exits with the following values:

       _0
           success

       _2
           invalid command syntax

       _3
           invalid argument to option

       _4
           GID is already used (when called without --oo)

       _9
           group name is already used

       _1_0
           can't update group file

